  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Stany ostrzegawcze (the hydrological bulletin)",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"Stany ostrzegawcze\" (the hydrological bulletin)**\n\nRisk Miner reads investment risk the way Poland reads a river: a level on a gauge staff, measured against a warning mark (stan ostrzegawczy, the base's P50) and an alarm mark (stan alarmowy, P80). The world is an IMGW bulletin: a deep river-navy shell, a light mist work ground, water blue as the only colour that acts, and a three-step warning ladder that carries risk level and nothing else.\n\nDensity is that of a working desk tool, not a marketing page: white sheets on mist, condensed numerals, every figure tabular. The signature is the gauge staff (wodowskaz), a decimetre E-block face on a navy plate that water fills once and settles at P80. The same level vocabulary colours chips, tiles, chart dots, chart bands and the small staff bar in the project drawer, so a reader who has learned one gauge can read every screen.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "River-navy rail and gauge plates; mist ground; white sheets.",
      "Water blue for every action, link, selection and focus on light ground.",
      "Green / yellow / red ladder for risk level only, always tied to P50 and P80.",
      "Barlow for prose, Barlow Semi Condensed for headings and numbers, tabular figures everywhere.",
      "Motion fills and settles once; reduced motion shows the final state."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Water Rule",
        "body": "Water is the only action colour. Buttons, links, active tabs, focus rings on light ground and selected rows are water; no ladder colour ever marks something clickable.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ladder Means Level Rule",
        "body": "Green, yellow and red say where a value stands against the base's P50 and P80, and nothing else. Pending, to-verify, in-progress and info states use water wash, never the ladder.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Marks Rule",
        "body": "P50 is always stan ostrzegawczy (yellow) and P80 always stan alarmowy (red), in gauges, chart bands, staff bars, legends and copy. `levelOf` is the single source: value ≥ P80 alarm, ≥ P50 warn, otherwise ok.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` is set on body and inherited everywhere; numbers align in columns and do not jitter while counting up.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Condensed Numbers Rule",
        "body": "Every figure that is read as a measurement, and every heading, sets in Barlow Semi Condensed; prose and controls stay in Barlow.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Kicker Rule",
        "body": "Headings stand alone; no small label line sits above a heading or a value. A tile names itself with a run-in label on the same line as its value, never with a caption stacked above it. The only uppercase text in the system is the nav group label inside the river rail.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use water (`--water`) for every action and link, and water wash for pending, to-verify and info states.",
      "Do colour risk by `levelOf`: below P50 ok, P50 stan ostrzegawczy, P80 stan alarmowy, and label the marks with those words.",
      "Do say which measure a level colour shows (cost or time) wherever both could apply, and keep chart labels in the margin, off the data.",
      "Do set headings and measured figures in Barlow Semi Condensed with tabular figures.",
      "Do keep motion to one fill-and-settle, count-up or slide, on `cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`, and show final states under `prefers-reduced-motion`.",
      "Do tint every shadow and scrim with river navy."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use green, yellow or red for anything other than risk level against P50/P80.",
      "Don't add a second action hue or make a ladder colour clickable.",
      "Don't put uppercase kickers or eyebrows above headings.",
      "Don't loop animations; nothing pulses, waves or spins indefinitely.",
      "Don't fall back to system display faces or proportional figures for numbers."
    ]
  }
}